All about image resources in recovery images
============================================

The code in "messages.sh" depends on a collection of PNG files
stored in /etc/screens in the initramfs file system.  The images are
generated by the "make_images" and "make_text_images" scripts.


Contents of /etc/screens, and what it's for
===========================================

screens/<locale>/<msgname>.png
  These are the localized individual message files displayed during
  recovery.  <locale> is a standard locale name (e.g. "en", "fr",
  "zh_CN", etc.).  <msgname> is the name used in "messages.sh".

  The message source text for for each ".png" file can be found in
  "localized_text/<locale>/<msgname>.txt".

screens/progress_box.png
screens/progress_increment.png
  Images used for the progress bar in `progress_bar`.  The
  "progress_box" image shows a progress bar at 0% completion.  The
  "progress_increment" image is a column of pixels corresponding to
  a 1% increment.

screens/moving_box_??.png
  While we're installing the recovery image, we present an animation
  of a box that moves back and forth across the screen.  These files
  represent the individual frames of that animation.  Their generation
  is described in detail in comments in "make_image".

The message images are generated at build time from the text sources.
The non-message images are checked in to the git repository, although
they can be regenerated from scratch, if need be.


Adding a new locale
===================

All available locales are currently stored under localized_text;
however, not all locales are included in the final output.  Each
Chrome computer is manufactured to be used in a specific locale;
the 'screens' locale content is only generated for the locales to
which units may actually be shipped.

The list of supported locales is stored in a global setting at the
top of 'make_text_images'.  A new locale can be added by updating
that setting.

Note that you should not accept on faith that simply updating the
list of supported locales is enough to produce a good set of images.
A number of things may go wrong:
  * The text layout may not wrap lines properly, causing text to be
    truncated or otherwise mis-formatted.
  * Text may be truncated for lack of space.
  * Writing systems that display right-to-left are untested, and may
    not render properly.  Most notable in this class are 'ar'
    (Arabic) and 'iw' (Hebrew).  Give these special attention
    when/if they're added.
  * Some fonts may be missing or incomplete.
  * The translators didn't have access to a Chromebook to observe
    the messages in context, and may have mis-translated some
    messages.
  * Translators are human, and sometimes make mistakes.

When adding a new locale, you should at minimum visually inspect the
images to confirm that it looks like the original text.  If you can,
it would be wise to find someone familiar with the target language
to confirm there are no glaring errors.


How to generate content
=======================

To generate just the non-text images, invoke "make_images" with no
arguments.  This will re-generate all graphic images from scratch.
Normally, you shouldn't need to do this unless you make a change to
"make_images".

The text content for initramfs images is generated automatically in
the ebuild; if your only change is text messages, you don't need to
do anything.  However, you may want to generate all images prior to
building in order to review the images directly (e.g. using a
browser to display the files).  For convenience, the 'make_images'
command can also generate the text images.

To generate graphic images and messages for all supported locales:
  ./make_images --all

To generate graphic images and messages for specified locales:
  ./make_images <locale> [ <locale> ... ]

The second form can be used to generate images for locales that
aren't part of the default supported set.
